      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Angioedema attributable to acquired C1 inhibitor (C1-INH) deficiency is a rare disease related to lymphoproliferative disorders or autoantibodies to Cl inhibitor. We describe a patient with angioedema and autoantibodies to C1 inhibitor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study the characteristics of autoantibodies to C1-INH in a patient with acquired angioedema."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Autoantibodies to Cl-INH were measured by enzyme-linked immunoadsorbent assay. Immunoglobulin (Ig)G autoantibody was purified by affinity chromatography on a protein G agarose column. We developed an enzyme-linked immunoadsorbent assay to determine whether the autoantibodies were directed against the C1-INH active center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "IgM and mainly C1-INH IgG autoantibodies were detected; both had kappa and lambda chains. No monoclonal component was detected. The autoantibodies were directed against the Cl-INH active center. After various treatment strategies were attempted, an effective clinical response was attained with antifibrinolytic therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "A case of acquired angioedema because of C1-INH deficiency was found to be attributable to the presence of polyclonal autoantibodies to C1-INH."
        }
      ]},
